# Python Standards (3.12+)

## Type Hints
- All function signatures must include type hints for parameters and return values
- Use `from __future__ import annotations` at top of files with forward references
- Use `typing` module types: Optional, Union, List, Dict, Tuple, Callable, Any
- Use `pathlib.Path` exclusively — never use os.path string manipulation

## Dataclasses & Models
- Use `@dataclass` or `@dataclass(frozen=True)` for all model/data container classes
- Use `__slots__` on performance-critical classes
- Never use plain dicts to pass structured data between functions — define a model

## Concurrency
- All file I/O and scanning operations run in QThread (not main thread)
- Use PyQt6 signals/slots for thread-to-GUI communication — never directly update GUI from worker threads
- Use threading.Event for cancellation signals to worker threads
- Log thread name in all threaded log entries

## Logging
- Use Python's logging module exclusively — no print() statements in production code
- Log levels: DEBUG for trace, INFO for progress, WARNING for skipped/flagged, ERROR for failures
- Every module gets its own logger: `logger = logging.getLogger(__name__)`
- Log format: `[%(asctime)s] [%(levelname)s] [%(name)s] %(message)s`

## Imports & Dependencies
- Use absolute imports only
- Pin all dependencies in requirements.txt with exact versions
- Group: stdlib, third-party, local — blank line between groups
- Never import * from any module

## Code Quality
- Use f-strings exclusively for string formatting
- Use list/dict/set comprehensions where readable
- Use pathlib.Path / operators for path joining
- Prefer `with` statements for all file operations
- Use `enum.Enum` for all state/status constants
- No magic numbers — all numeric constants named and stored in config/constants.py
